  "body": {
    "id": "thm:orbit-rank-granularity",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every fixed \\(G\\leq G_\\star\\), every fixed statistic \\(T:\\mathcal X_{m,K}\\to\\mathbb R\\), and every \\(C\\in\\mathcal X_{m,K}\\), the conventional nonrandomized group-rank p-value satisfies \\(p_G(C)\\in\\{1/2,1\\}\\) and \\(\\Pr_R\\{p_G(C)\\leq\\alpha\\}\\leq\\alpha\\) for every \\(R\\in\\mathcal R_0(n,K)\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "G",
      "Gstar",
      "Tstat",
      "X",
      "m",
      "K",
      "C",
      "pG",
      "alpha",
      "P",
      "R",
      "R0",
      "n"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:balanced-complete-randomization",
      "def:legal-response-tables",
      "def:profile-space",
      "def:orbit-rank-pvalue",
      "thm:maximal-common-group"
    ],
    "justification": "The theorem states the exact inferential consequence of maximal common symmetry and no broader impossibility claim.",
    "gap": "The rank argument is informed by \\cite{HemerikGoeman2018}; the substantive input is the newly derived orbit-size frontier from the alphabet-frontier theorem.",
    "consumer": "For the binary reply audit, it shows why conventional common-orbit ranks cannot reproduce the usual \\(5\\%\\) HC2 or robust-\\(t\\) workflow studied by \\cite{ZhaoDing2021}.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(G\\leq G_\\star\\), a statistic \\(T\\), and a profile \\(C\\).  Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}maximal\\text{-}common\\text{-}group}\\) shows that every \\(G\\)-orbit has one or two points.  If the orbit of \\(C\\) is a singleton, every \\(f\\in G\\) fixes \\(C\\), and Definition \\(\\mathrm{def{:}orbit\\text{-}rank\\text{-}pvalue}\\) gives \\(p_G(C)=1\\).  If its orbit is \\(\\{C,C^*\\}\\), the orbit--stabilizer counting bijection gives exactly \\(|G|/2\\) elements sending \\(C\\) to each of \\(C\\) and \\(C^*\\).  Because \\(T(C)\\geq T(C)\\),\n\\[\n p_G(C)=\n \\begin{cases}\n  1/2,&T(C^*)<T(C),\\\\\n  1,&T(C^*)\\geq T(C).\n \\end{cases}                                             \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThus \\(p_G(C)\\in\\{1/2,1\\}\\) for every profile, including for the trivial subgroup.\n\nNow fix a legal response table \\(R\\).  By the defining property of \\(G_\\star\\),\n\\[\n P_R(f(C))=P_R(C)\\qquad(f\\in G).                         \\tag{2}\n\\]\nConsequently the random profile is uniform conditional on each positive-probability \\(G\\)-orbit: (2) assigns the same mass to every point of that orbit.  If \\(0\\leq\\alpha<1/2\\), (1) makes the rejection event empty.  If \\(1/2\\leq\\alpha<1\\), a singleton orbit contributes no rejection, and a two-point orbit contributes at most one of its two equally likely points, because both points cannot strictly exceed one another under \\(T\\).  Therefore, summing over the disjoint orbits,\n\\[\n \\Pr_R\\{p_G(C)\\leq\\alpha\\}\\leq\\frac12\\leq\\alpha.     \\tag{3}\n\\]\nFor \\(\\alpha=1\\), the probability is \\(1\\).  Equations (1)--(3) prove both the granularity and the finite-sample validity claimed in the theorem, using only the balanced design probability kernel in Assumption \\(\\mathrm{ass{:}balanced\\text{-}complete\\text{-}randomization}\\).",
